        "target": "Given the problem, I first identified the key quantities and the bridge's weight limit. The bridge can hold up to 100 kilograms. The weights of the three children were provided: Kelly weighs 34 kilograms, which is 15% less than Megan, and Mike weighs 5 kilograms more than Megan. My task was to determine how much the three children weigh together and how much this exceeds the bridge's weight limit.\n\nI started by calculating Megan's weight. Since Kelly weighs 34 kilograms, which is 15% less than Megan, I used the equation:\n\\[ \\text{Kelly's weight} = \\text{Megan's weight} \\times (1 - 0.15) \\]\n\\[ 34 = \\text{Megan's weight} \\times 0.85 \\]\nSolving for Megan's weight:\n\\[ \\text{Megan's weight} = \\frac{34}{0.85} = 40 \\text{ kilograms} \\]\n\nNext, I calculated Mike's weight. Since Mike weighs 5 kilograms more than Megan:\n\\[ \\text{Mike's weight} = \\text{Megan's weight} + 5 = 40 + 5 = 45 \\text{ kilograms} \\]\n\nNow, I summed the weights of the three children:\n\\[ \\text{Total weight} = \\text{Kelly's weight} + \\text{Megan's weight} + \\text{Mike's weight} = 34 + 40 + 45 = 119 \\text{ kilograms} \\]\n\nTo find how much the total weight exceeds the bridge's limit, I subtracted the bridge's weight limit from the total weight:\n\\[ \\text{Excess weight} = \\text{Total weight} - \\text{Bridge's weight limit} = 119 - 100 = 19 \\text{ kilograms} \\]\n\nThe operation I would reuse is the calculation of the total weight of the three children and the comparison of this total to the bridge's weight limit. This can be applied to similar problems involving the sum of individual weights and a given limit.\n\nFINAL: 19",
        "target_sha256": "062af3e4573aeceec14abe2d1cd8864b9057ce1aacbbb6b7d09364d27b59e1d3"
