        "student_prefix_sha256": "9d7c64c8cef6c2705d7f174d8bd3ad9f1dba47520f58efd04edaa98f0ae84ae4",
        "target": "Given the initial deposit of $2000 and an annual interest rate of 8%, I knew the interest would be withdrawn immediately after it was deposited. The task was to find the total amount after 2.5 years. I identified the key quantities: the principal ($2000), the annual interest rate (8%), and the time period (2.5 years).\n\nFirst, I calculated the annual interest:\n\\[ 2000 \\times 0.08 = 160 \\text{ dollars} \\]\n\nSince the interest is withdrawn each year, the principal remains $2000 for each of the first two years. For the first two years, the total interest earned would be:\n\\[ 160 \\times 2 = 320 \\text{ dollars} \\]\n\nFor the half year, the interest would be:\n\\[ 160 \\times 0.5 = 80 \\text{ dollars} \\]\n\nAdding the interest from the first two years and the half year, the total interest earned is:\n\\[ 320 + 80 = 400 \\text{ dollars} \\]\n\nAdding this interest to the initial deposit, the total amount after 2.5 years is:\n\\[ 2000 + 400 = 2400 \\text{ dollars} \\]\n\nTo verify, I checked that the interest calculation for each year and the half year was correct and that the total interest was properly summed. The operations used were straightforward multiplication and addition, which are reliable and reusable for similar problems.\n\nI would reuse this method for any problem involving simple interest where the interest is withdrawn immediately after it is deposited.\n\nFINAL: 2400",
